The operator 𝒯i\mathcal{T}\_{i} defines the one-step backward recursion of the convertible bond value and integrates three key contractual features. First, when the bond call condition is satisfied, i.e., Ci​(x)=1C\_{i}(x)=1, the issuer has the right to exercise the call option. When Ki<Vicont​(x)K\_{i}<V^{\mathrm{cont}}\_{i}(x), the issuer exercises the call option and the bond value is immediately determined by the call price KiK\_{i}, corresponding to an optimal stopping event. However, prior to such forced redemption, the bondholder may either continue holding the bond or convert to stocks. Second, when the reset condition is triggered, i.e., Ritrig​(x)=1R\_{i}^{\mathrm{trig}}(x)=1, the conversion price is adjusted according to the reset rule. Finally, in the continuation region 𝒞\mathcal{C} where neither condition is met, the bond value is equal to the discounted conditional expected next-step value plus coupon payments, consistent with the standard risk-neutral valuation principle. The priority of call provision ensures that the stopping region dominates the switching region when both conditions are simultaneously activated.

