In the literature, estimating conditional expectations by neural networks in a backward induction has been investigated in the context of finite-horizon stochastic control problems in Huré et al. ([2021](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.12189#bib.bib21)). Such “deep LSMC” approach has recently been applied to option hedging (Fécamp et al., [2021](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.12189#bib.bib17); Bachouch et al., [2022](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.12189#bib.bib5)), storage optimization (Bachouch et al., [2022](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.12189#bib.bib5); Warin, [2023](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.12189#bib.bib38)), portfolio optimization (Franco et al., [2022](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.12189#bib.bib19); Roch, [2023](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.12189#bib.bib35)), and utility maximization (Arandjelović et al., [2026](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.12189#bib.bib3)), but has so far never been used for the pricing of convertible bonds with path-dependent provisions.

